The switch of native-macos.yml to the Rust + Tauri build, prepared by M-I for the 0.5 merge into main (mac-plan.md M-I, D14) and not
applied before it: the Swift app keeps releasing 0.4.x from main until then. Apply from the repository root with
    git apply native/tauri/release/native-macos.yml.0.5.patch
Written against main's native-macos.yml of 66e42410/b7e7f6f5, whose design it keeps: changes, macos-tests and notarize-macos
(notarize-macos.sh on the new app, started once the build is done) are unchanged, and so are the runner rule (the self-hosted
mighty-mac for main pushes and dispatches, macos-latest for pull requests and branches, the `hosted` dispatch input), the caches on
GitHub's runners only, ad-hoc CI signing, the artifact names (MAC_ZIP_ARTIFACT: MightyCode-native-macos / -unsigned) and the size gate.
Only two jobs change:
- macos builds with scripts/build-tauri-macos.sh (MightyCode.app, release/MightyCode-macos.zip, the browser engine on main pushes).
  It never opens the app's window: its only runs of the binary are the headless --verify-resources (as build-macos.sh's) and Swift's
  stager (tools/parity-mac stage) reading the zip. Rust is installed and cached on GitHub's runners only; on mighty-mac the
  developer's own toolchain is used, never changed.
- macos-smoke (GitHub's runner, the built zip) runs the MCP servers' check, the Tauri smoke in place of the Swift app's six smokes,
  and the Rust app's help capture.
native-tauri.yml checks that this patch applies to origin/main's native-macos.yml (git apply --check).

